-- title:
--   Primes over varpi at an end of the blown-up chart
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime, $M'$ a nonzero natural number with $q \nmid M'$, and $\ell$ a prime with $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell \mid M'$. Further, $L$ is a field of characteristic $0$, $\zeta \in L$ a primitive $q$-th root of unity, and the hypothesis `hι` asks that some ring homomorphism $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ send $\zeta$ to $e^{2\pi i/q}$. The subgroup $H_1 \le (\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times$ is, by `hH₁`, the intersection of [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH q M'`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelJacobian.html#L22), the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$, with the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/\ell)^\times$; and $K$ is, by `hK`, the intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}(L)$ obtained by adjoining to $L$ the coefficientwise image (under [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81), induced by $\mathbb{Q} \to L$) of the $q$-expansion function field [`ModularCurve.xHFunctionField (q^2*M') H₁`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L79) of the congruence group [`CohCarrier.GammaH (q^2*M') H₁`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L133).
--
--   The base ring $A$ is a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$, henselian local, with algebraically closed residue field, with $q$ in its maximal ideal and with $\zeta$ in the image of $A \to L$; $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$. The element $j \in K$ is nonzero and has Laurent expansion the image of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) of the modular invariant. Moreover $\varpi$ generates the maximal ideal of $A$ (`hϖ`), and $\varpi_t \in A$ satisfies $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1} = q\,u$ for a unit $u$ (`hϖt`).
--
--   Write $C =$ `chartAlgFin A K j`, the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ of elements integral over $A[j]$, and $j_C =$ `jChartFin A K j` for $j$ regarded in $C$. The ideal $y \subseteq C$ is maximal, contains the image of $\varpi$, and is supersingular in the sense of `hss`: for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : C \to \Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(j_C)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), i.e. every elliptic curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no nonzero $q$-torsion point. Let $X$ = [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236), the pushout gluing $\operatorname{Spec} C$ to the chart at infinity $\operatorname{Spec}$ `chartAlgInf A K j`, with structure morphism `toBase` to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and with `ιFin` the morphism from $X_{\mathrm{Fin}} = \operatorname{Spec} C$ into $X$.
--
--   The hypothesis `hArig` (universally quantified crossing presentation, its conclusion summarised here in seven groups) asserts: for every point $z$ of $X$ such that the germ $\varpi_z$ at $z$ of the image of $\varpi$ under `toBase` lies in the maximal ideal of the stalk $\mathcal{O}_{X,z}$, and every point $y'$ of $X_{\mathrm{Fin}}$ mapping to $z$ whose ideal satisfies the same supersingularity condition as $y$ above, there exist a complete discrete valuation domain $W$, a ring homomorphism $\sigma : A \to W$ with $\mathfrak{m}_W = (\sigma\varpi)$, power series $f, u, v \in W[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u, v$ units and $f \equiv X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$ (the Drinfeld form [`DrinfeldCurve.LocalChart.drinfeldForm`](def/DrinfeldCurve_LocalChart.html#L18)), and a ring isomorphism $e$ from the $\mathfrak{m}$-adic completion of $\mathcal{O}_{X,z}$ onto $S = W[[X_0,X_1]]/\bigl(C(\sigma(\varpi_t^{q+1}))\,v - f\,u\bigr)$, such that, with $toC$ the completion map, $mkS$ the quotient map and $germY$ the germ map $C \to \mathcal{O}_{X,z}$ through `ιFin`: (i) $e$ carries the germ of $a \in A$ to $C(\sigma a)$; (ii) every automorphism $\tau$ of $K/L$ which is a level automorphism attached to $\gamma^{-1}$ for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ — in the sense of [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L q ζ q (q^2*M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29), namely that for all quotients $f/g$ of modular forms of level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ with integral $q$-expansions representing an element $x \in K$, the coefficientwise image under any $\iota$ with $\iota\zeta = e^{2\pi i/q}$ of the expansion of $\tau x$ agrees with the ratio of the $q$-expansions of $f$ and $g$ slashed by `conjElemN q γ⁻¹` — preserves $C$; (iii) if in addition $\gamma_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod{\ell}$ then such a $\tau$ acts trivially on $C/y'$; (iv) for such a $\tau$ acting trivially on $C/y'$ there are a ring automorphism $\theta$ of $S$, an element $c \in W$ and a matrix $M \in M_2(W)$ with $\theta$ compatible with $\tau$ through $e \circ toC \circ germY$, $\theta$ fixing constants, $\theta(X_{j}) \equiv \sum_i M_{ij}X_i$ modulo the square of the ideal generated by the images of $X_0, X_1$, $c^{q+1} \equiv 1$, $M \equiv c\,\gamma$ entrywise modulo $\mathfrak{m}_W$, $c \equiv 1$ whenever $\gamma_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod{\ell}$, and $c \not\equiv 1$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau \neq \mathrm{id}$; (v) two primes of $S$ omitting one of the images of $X_0, X_1$, containing $\sigma\varpi$ and containing elements with linear terms $a_iX_0 + b_iX_1$ with $q \nmid a_1b_2 - a_2b_1$, have distinct contractions to $\mathcal{O}_{X,z}$; (vi) for a prime $P$ of $S$ of that kind containing an element with linear term $X_0$, an element $a \in C$ has $toC(germY(a))$ in the contraction of $P$ exactly when every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in the image of the maximal ideal of $A$; (vii) the element [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L q`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) lies in $K$ and in $C$, is congruent modulo $y'$ to some $a_0 \in A$, and the image of that difference under $e \circ toC \circ germY$ is $mkS(h)$ for some $h \in (X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$, $e_0 \ge 1$, whose degree-$e_0$ leading form takes unit values at all $(a,b) \in W^2$ with $a$ or $b$ a unit and $a^qb - ab^q \in \mathfrak{m}_W$.
--
--   One such point is fixed: $z$, the germ $\varpi_z$ of $\varpi$ lying in the maximal ideal of $\mathcal{O}_{X,z}$, a point $y'$ of $X_{\mathrm{Fin}}$ over $z$ satisfying the supersingularity condition and with $y'$ the point given by $y$ (`hy'y`); and a presentation $W_1, \sigma_1, f_1, u_1, v_1, e_1$ of the above shape is given, together with the hypothesis `hW₁`, which is the list (i)–(vii) just described for this data.
--
--   Next, `hJ` defines the centre $J \subseteq C$ as the infimum of the ideals $\tau^{-1}\bigl((e_1 \circ toC \circ germY)^{-1}(\,(mkS(C(\sigma_1\varpi_t)), mkS(X_0), mkS(X_1))\,)\bigr)$, taken over $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and level automorphisms $\tau$ attached to $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $C$. The subalgebra $B \subseteq K$ is, by `hB`, the $A$-subalgebra generated over $C$ by $\{x \in K : \exists\, i \in J,\ x\varpi_t = i\}$, i.e. the $\varpi_t$-chart of the blow-up of $J$. A valuation subring $W$ of $K$ is given with $B \subseteq W$ (`hBW`), and: `hR1` says $C \subseteq B$ and every element of $K$ is a ratio of elements of $B$ with nonzero denominator; `hR2` says $B$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $A$ and $B/\varpi B$ has Krull dimension at most $1$; `hR3` (five clauses) says that an element of $L$ lies in $W$ iff it comes from $A$, that $\mathfrak{m}_W$ is generated by the image of $\varpi$, that $W$ is a discrete valuation ring, that for $b \in C$ one has $b \in y$ iff $b$ lies in $\mathfrak{m}_W$, and that $W$ is the localisation of $B$ at $\mathfrak{m}_W \cap B$. The hypothesis `hEQ` (five clauses, summarised here) provides: for every $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$-algebra structure on the residue field $k$ of $A$, a surjection $\rho : B \to$ [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q k`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) with kernel $\mathfrak{m}_W \cap B$, compatible with $A \to k$, and equivariant for the level automorphisms $\tau$ attached to $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W$, through an element $c \in \mathbb{F}_{q^2}^\times$ with $(\,$`redQ q γ`$, c)$ in [`DrinfeldCurve.hSubgroup q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L276) acting by [`DrinfeldCurve.hAction`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L325), with $c \neq 1$ when $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau \neq \mathrm{id}$; stability of $B$ under all such $\tau$; that every prime of $B$ containing $\varpi$ is the image of $\mathfrak{m}_W \cap B$ under some $\tau$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$; that an element of $B$ sent into $\mathfrak{m}_W$ by all such $\tau$ is divisible by $\varpi$; and that a $\tau$ preserving $y$ on $C$ preserves $W$.
--
--   Finally $O$ is a subring of $K$ subject to `hO`: there are $a \in J$ with nonzero image in $K$, a maximal ideal $P$ of the $A$-subalgebra $B_a$ generated over $C$ by $\{x \in K : \exists\, i \in J,\ xa = i\}$, such that $O$ is the localisation of $B_a$ at $P$ (an element of $K$ lies in $O$ iff it is $g/h$ with $g, h \in B_a$, $h \notin P$), every element of $y$ that lies in $O$ is a non-unit of $O$, and $B \not\subseteq O$. Thus $O$ is an end of the blown-up chart lying over $y$ but not dominated by $B$.
--
--   The conclusion has two parts. First, $O \subseteq W$. Secondly, there exist prime ideals $\mathfrak{q}_E \neq \mathfrak{q}_I$ of $O$ such that: for every $f$ lying in both $O$ and $W$, $f \in \mathfrak{q}_E$ if and only if $f \in \mathfrak{m}_W$ (so $\mathfrak{q}_E$ is the contraction of $\mathfrak{m}_W$); if the image of $\varpi$ lies in $O$ then it lies in $\mathfrak{q}_I$; there is an element $b \in y$ whose image, whenever it lies in $O$, does not lie in $\mathfrak{q}_I$; there is a non-unit of $O$ outside $\mathfrak{q}_E$; there is a non-unit of $O$ outside $\mathfrak{q}_I$; and every prime $\mathfrak{q}$ of $O$ containing the image of $\varpi$ (whenever that image lies in $O$) and distinct from both $\mathfrak{q}_E$ and $\mathfrak{q}_I$ contains all non-units of $O$, i.e. is the maximal ideal.
--
--   This is the local trichotomy at an end of the blown-up supersingular chart of the integral model: over $\varpi$ the local ring $O$ of such an end has exactly three primes containing $\varpi$, namely its maximal ideal, the exceptional one cut out by the valuation ring $W$, and one further Igusa-type prime missing the supersingular ideal $y$. It is the variant of the analysis for the auxiliary level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ with a guard prime $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$, and is used in the study of primes of the integral model over supersingular points and in the construction of Igusa-type valuation rings not dominated by the exceptional chart.
